          "quote": "# Two-hundred-first pass: one current prose edition\n\n## Unified release\n\nThe four normalized sub-editions now form one continuous current prose reader:\n\n- 381 visible events;\n- 380 logical source tokens after the one line-edge read-once correction;\n- 135 fields;\n- 116 translated statements;\n- eleven records on seven fixed prose pages;\n- 173 exact cards with 230 unambiguous registered surfaces.\n\nEvery event has a visible form, normalized master form, exact card, short value,\nfield mode, owner and close status. Every statement has a literal card sequence\nand a fluent German working translation.\n\n## Dictionary architecture\n\nThe compact component/codebook architecture joins exactly to the current cards:\n\n| layer | card types | events |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| productive composition | 151 | 353 |\n| learned whole card | 22 | 28 |\n\nThis is a workable small-shop system. Most running text is built from reusable\ncomponents; the memorized deck is only twenty-two cards, several of which share\nshort teaching heads such as vessel, clear run or previous item. The 57 written\naliases are handled by the ten-rule reader sheet.\n\n## Important editorial warning\n\nThe new dictionary deliberately keeps two columns: the current card value and\nthe older component-closure reading snapshot. The current value controls the\nnew translations. The snapshot is not silently treated as current because it\nexposes unresolved historical conflicts, notably:\n\n- `tchody`: final addition versus cool/store;\n- `qekey`: brief processing versus raw;\n- `dchey`: base part versus root;\n- `sh`: hold versus stalk;\n- `dshedy`: short settle versus fresh water.\n\nThese are now the highest-value next targets. A compact audit of all twenty-two\nwhole cards should choose one short default for each from their complete local\noccurrences and visible owners. No new component is needed until that deck is\ninternally consistent.",
